The '''Barrows''' is a series of six burial mounds east of Mort'ton in which the [[Barrows brothers]] were entombed after their deaths during the [[Third Age]]. 'Barrows' also commonly refers to the minigame-like dungeon-delving experience of looting [[Chest (Barrows)|the chest]] at the centre of the tunnels beneath the crypts, released on [[9 May]] [[2005]].

Players can attempt to rob valuable items from the Barrows brothers' crypts. However, due to an ancient spell, the brothers' [[wight]]s continued to dwell in the barrows, protecting these treasures.

Looting the Barrows is popular due to the potential of receiving valuable rewards, including the unique [[Barrows equipment]], with increasingly valuable rewards for killing more monsters in the crypts, including the [[Barrows brothers]] themselves.

In the [[Miniquests|miniquest]] [[His Faithful Servants]] players are tasked to slay all six [[Barrows brothers]] and then loot the chest for the [[strange icon]]. Completing the miniquest rewards 20,000 Prayer experience and the ability to use the [[minimap]] within the [[Barrows tunnels]].

==History==
The [[Barrows brothers]] were a band of six legendary [[Saradomin]]ist warriors from a small town in [[Misthalin]]{{CiteVideo|author=Old School RuneScape|title=The Story That Leads to the Finale... {{!}} Blood Moon Rises, An Illustrated Myreque Questline|url=FxlCNckevMc?t=426|timestamp=7:06|date=|site=|notes=|name=}} who ventured into [[Morytania]] during the [[God Wars]], leading the [[Morytania Campaign]]. They had been provided power, weapons and armour by the [[Zaros]]ian [[mahjarrat]] [[Sliske]], enabling them to push deep into [[Morytania]].{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=A Stranger Watches|quote=it was those very weapons and armours he had given them, that they now wielded, which granted them their desire.}}

As the campaign pressed on through the swamps, and neared its end, Sliske, who had followed the campaign from its beginning,{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=A Stranger Watches|quote=The stranger had watched the campaign from its first footfall into the lands east of the river|name=footfall}} decided to finally relinquish his "blessing". As the Saradominists reached the walls of Drakan's city of [[Darkmeyer]], the Mahjarrat appeared to them, saying that it was time for them to begin their servitude to him, but first, they must die.{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|quote=The time has come, my warriors. The gifts I bestowed upon you have served you well, and you, in turn, have served your god. Now, it is time to begin your servitude to me... to my god. You shall stand ever vigilant for His return, and then you will be champions in His glorious army - we shall have victory against the usurper. But first, you must die...|sectitle=The Pact, Fulfilled}} He then simply walked out of the camp, leaving the brothers and onlooking soldiers in shock.

After this meeting, the brothers continued their campaign; however, they no longer fought with the same strength and skill,{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=Ruination|quote=Yet more battles followed that fated night ... The brothers continued to fight, as heroes among men, yet they slowed, they erred. ... They bruised and bled, requiring tending from their medics after each skirmish.}} and Sliske's continued presence at every battle seemed to be the cause of the brothers' missteps.{{CiteText|text=Crumbling tome|quote=The most disturbing thing was how his presence started to affect the brothers, they became distracted and on occasion even received injuries that required attention.|page=6}} Soon, Lord Drakan's forces launched a powerful assault against the brothers and their soldiers while they were camped deep inside Morytania.{{CiteText|text=Crumbling tome|quote=It was deep in the heart of the enemies' territory, in the failing light of dusk, that the enemy struck back.|page=6-7}} Many of the Saradominist forces were killed, but Lord Drakan's army was eventually forced to retreat.{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=Ruination|quote=Many men fell around them, yet, despite their losses, together they managed to set the enemy to retreat, striking a heavy blow in return.}} The brothers were mortally wounded in this battle,{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=Ruination|quote=All morn and eve, they were tended upon once more, but this time they did not recover. Muttering feverishly, their wounds did not close and their breathing weakened.}} and by the evening of the following day, they all had died.{{CiteText|text=Crumbling tome|quote=As if on cue all the six brothers sighed one last breath and died|page=9}}

The brothers were buried in six barrows at the site where their camp had stood.{{CiteText|text=Crumbling tome|quote=On the last day the assembled troops buried the brothers inside their stone tombs, then with great reverence, but little ceremony, they covered the tombs with earth.|page=12}} During the construction of the tombs, Lord Drakan's forces refrained from attacking and instead taunted the surviving soldiers incessantly every night.{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=Ruination|quote=for six nights, their enemies surrounded the camp, chiding and braying, mocking the loss of their leaders, breaking their spirit but not attacking.}} 

After the burial was complete, the remaining officers recognised that the campaign was doomed to fail without its leaders, and the surviving soldiers soon retreated back across the [[River Salve]].{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=Ruination|quote=The remaining officers came to the realisation that without their leaders they were lost and must now turn back if there were to be any hope of survival}}{{CiteText|text=Crumbling tome|quote=the army broke camp and turned to march back to their lands}} Many of these soldiers would perish on the journey back to Misthalin.{{CiteText|text=Crumbling tome|quote=Not many of the original army made it back to these lands, many more had perished on the journey home|page=13}} As the Saradominist army retreated, Sliske returned to the Barrows and transformed the six dead brothers into [[wight]]s.{{CiteLore|title=The Fall of Six|sectitle=Ruination|quote=Arms outstretched atop the central mound, the stranger chanted in some foul, ancient language, casting his old magic. A vulgar purple light formed in front of him, fell to the earth, and bled into each of the barrows.}} At some point, he tasked them with guarding the [[strange icon]].{{CiteNPC|npc=Strange Old Man|loc=His Faithful Servants|quote=They guard it for him! That was their purpose}}

==Transportation==
[[File:Barrows Map.png|thumb|right|295px|
1. (Red) Using the [[Barrows Teleport]] ([[Barrows teleport (tablet)|tab]]) is the quickest route.<br/>
2. (Blue, Green and Orange) Using the [[Minigame Teleport|Mort'ton minigame teleport]], [[Mort'ton teleport|Mort'ton teleport scroll]] and [[Morytania legs 3]] & [[Morytania legs 4|4]] are good alternatives to players who don't have access to the Barrows teleport.<br/>
3. (Pink and Light blue) Travelling using the [[Swamp Boaty]] is a valid method for ironmen who don't have access to abovementioned options.<br/>
4. (Yellow) Using the {{Fairycode|BIP}} fairy ring is the longest of the routes, but takes the player close to the [[Nature Grotto]], which players can visit to restore their prayer points.]]

There are several different methods for travelling to the Barrows. The best options, ordered from fast to slow, are:

*Casting [[Barrows Teleport]] in the [[Arceuus spellbook]] (requires level 83 [[Magic]]), or using a [[Barrows teleport (tablet)|Barrows teleport tablet]] or [[Portal Chamber|house portal]] to teleport right next to the entrance.
*Using the [[Mort'ton teleport]] scroll and then running east to Barrows.
*Using the [[Morytania legs 3]] or [[Morytania legs 4|4]] to [[Burgh de Rott]] and running north through [[Mort'ton]] towards Barrows.
*Using the [[Minigame Teleport]] to [[Shades of Mort'ton (minigame)|Shades of Mort'ton]] (requires completion of the [[Shades of Mort'ton]] quest). This method is free and takes up no inventory or equipment spaces, though there is a 20-minute cooldown between uses.
*Casting [[Kharyrll Teleport]] in the [[Ancient Magicks|Ancient spellbook]] (requires level 66 Magic and completion of [[Desert Treasure I]]), or using a [[Kharyrll teleport (tablet)|Kharyrll teleport tablet]] or house portal to teleport to [[Canifis]], then using the [[Myreque Hideout]] shortcut south of the pub and running south, crossing the rope bridge, continuing south and using the [[Swamp Boaty|swamp boaty]] to travel to Mort'ton, then running east.
*Using the [[Fairy rings|fairy ring]] code {{Fairycode|BKR}}, running south-east and using the swamp boaty to travel to Mort'ton, then running east.
*Using the fairy ring code {{Fairycode|bip}}, hopping across the [[Stepping stone (Mort Myre Swamp)|stepping stone]] shortcut (requires level 50 [[Agility]]), then going east and south, following the path through Mort'ton.
*Using [[Drakan's medallion]] to teleport to [[Ver Sinhaza]] (requires completion of [[A Taste of Hope]]), leaving through the western door, running south-west to board the [[Boat (Darkness of Hallowvale)|boat]] to Burgh de Rott, and running north through Mort'ton to Barrows.
*Running east from Canifis, then south through the swamp, turning east at the northmost hunter spot, looping around to the other nearby hunter spot, then running south-west to the swamp exit.
Non-[[Ironman Mode|Ironman]] players may attempt to visit a [[player-owned house]] on a [[house party]] world with a [[portal nexus]] for a quick and cheap teleport to Barrows or Canifis. Additionally, players with level 72 [[Agility]] can use the [[Dry stone wall (Barrows)|dry stone wall]] shortcut on the south west side of the mounds, just east of the bridge.

==Requirements==
*Completion of [[Priest in Peril]] is required to enter [[Morytania]].
*Starting [[Nature Spirit]] is required to enter the [[Mort Myre Swamp]] via the gate guarded by [[Ulizius]].
*Started [[His Faithful Servants]] to begin digging into the crypts.

